## Proof

**Setup.** For $a \in \mathbb{R}$, the Sobolev space $H^{a}(\mathbb{R}^{n})$ is the completion of $\mathcal{S}(\mathbb{R}^{n})$ with respect to the norm
$$\|u\|_{H^{a}}^{2} \;=\; \int_{\mathbb{R}^{n}} \langle \xi\rangle^{\,2a}\,|\widehat{u}(\xi)|^{2}\,d\xi, \qquad \langle \xi\rangle := (1+|\xi|^{2})^{1/2},$$
where $\widehat{u}$ is the Fourier transform. Equivalently, $u \in H^{a}$ iff $\langle \xi\rangle^{a}\widehat{u} \in L^{2}(\mathbb{R}^{n})$.

The Bessel-potential operator $\langle D\rangle^{s}$ is the Fourier multiplier
$$\widehat{\langle D\rangle^{s} u}(\xi) \;=\; \langle \xi\rangle^{s}\,\widehat{u}(\xi).$$
It is well-defined on $\mathcal{S}(\mathbb{R}^{n})$ and extends to bounded operators between Sobolev spaces as shown below.

---

**Step 1 — $\langle D\rangle^{s}: H^{s+t} \to H^{t}$ is an isometric isomorphism onto $H^{t}$.**

For $u \in H^{s+t}(\mathbb{R}^{n})$,
$$\|\langle D\rangle^{s} u\|_{H^{t}}^{2}
= \int_{\mathbb{R}^{n}} \langle \xi\rangle^{2t}\,\bigl|\langle \xi\rangle^{s}\widehat{u}(\xi)\bigr|^{2}\,d\xi
= \int_{\mathbb{R}^{n}} \langle \xi\rangle^{2(s+t)}\,|\widehat{u}(\xi)|^{2}\,d\xi
= \|u\|_{H^{s+t}}^{2}.$$

Hence $\langle D\rangle^{s}$ maps $H^{s+t}$ boundedly (indeed isometrically) into $H^{t}$, and is injective.

**Surjectivity.** Given $v \in H^{t}(\mathbb{R}^{n})$, set $u := \langle D\rangle^{-s} v$, i.e.
$$\widehat{u}(\xi) = \langle \xi\rangle^{-s}\widehat{v}(\xi).$$
Then
$$\|u\|_{H^{s+t}}^{2}
= \int \langle \xi\rangle^{2(s+t)}\,|\widehat{u}(\xi)|^{2}\,d\xi
= \int \langle \xi\rangle^{2(s+t)}\,\langle \xi\rangle^{-2s}\,|\widehat{v}(\xi)|^{2}\,d\xi
= \int \langle \xi\rangle^{2t}\,|\widehat{v}(\xi)|^{2}\,d\xi
= \|v\|_{H^{t}}^{2} < \infty,$$
so $u \in H^{s+t}$, and $\langle D\rangle^{s} u = v$. Thus $\langle D\rangle^{s}$ is surjective onto $H^{t}$.

Therefore $\langle D\rangle^{s}: H^{s+t}(\mathbb{R}^{n}) \to H^{t}(\mathbb{R}^{n})$ is a bijective isometry, hence an isomorphism of Hilbert spaces (in fact a unitary up to the identification of the two spaces).

---

**Step 2 — The inverse.**

The computation above shows that the inverse is the Fourier multiplier with symbol $\langle \xi\rangle^{-s}$, namely
$$\bigl(\langle D\rangle^{s}\bigr)^{-1} \;=\; \langle D\rangle^{-s} \;:\; H^{t}(\mathbb{R}^{n}) \xrightarrow{\;\sim\;} H^{s+t}(\mathbb{R}^{n}),$$
defined by $\widehat{\langle D\rangle^{-s} v}(\xi) = \langle \xi\rangle^{-s}\widehat{v}(\xi)$. The same norm calculation (with the roles of $s$ and $-s$ exchanged) shows $\langle D\rangle^{-s}: H^{t} \to H^{s+t}$ is itself an isometric isomorphism, and the two maps are mutually inverse:
$$\langle D\rangle^{s}\circ \langle D\rangle^{-s} = \mathrm{Id}_{H^{t}}, \qquad \langle D\rangle^{-s}\circ \langle D\rangle^{s} = \mathrm{Id}_{H^{s+t}}.$$

---

**Conclusion.** For every $s, t \in \mathbb{R}$, the operator $\langle D\rangle^{s}$ is an isometric isomorphism
$$\langle D\rangle^{s} \;:\; H^{s+t}(\mathbb{R}^{n}) \;\xrightarrow{\;\sim\;}\; H^{t}(\mathbb{R}^{n}),$$
with inverse $\langle D\rangle^{-s} \;:\; H^{t}(\mathbb{R}^{n}) \to H^{s+t}(\mathbb{R}^{n})$.

